                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "669800e4-4c87-332e-82ae-6f017e8eac8f",
                        "entry_id": "b4918fd1-3c27-3c4b-94e5-49cd2f309d25",
                        "glosses": [
                            "dużych rozmiarów wzór utworzony przez ludzi na powierzchni ziemi poprzez usypanie ze skał, ziemi lub innych trwałych elementów krajobrazu"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ad540af-3dc5-4a90-a926-baaafcbe88a4",
                                "sense_id": "669800e4-4c87-332e-82ae-6f017e8eac8f",
                                "text": "Przypuszcza się, że geoglif ten stworzyli Celtowie."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1.1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "13b7e596-d783-4a97-9a22-ad1aab914485",
                        "source_entry_id": "b4918fd1-3c27-3c4b-94e5-49cd2f309d25",
                        "source_sense_id": "669800e4-4c87-332e-82ae-6f017e8eac8f",
                        "target_lang_word": "geoglyph",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "geoglyph",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                        "entry_id": "36dfb7af-b9e9-3bf6-b3b2-f58f647ade58",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A large-scale drawing or image made on the ground by arranging lines of stones, scratching the earth, etc., and often only fully visible from a distance or the air."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cd844f1a-40a4-4347-858e-86504aa3c013",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "These, and almost 100 others stretched along the Colorado River in the California and Arizona deserts, are called geoglyphs or earth carvings by scientists. [...] Although their exact purpose and meaning are still a mystery, we do know these large and delicate geoglyphs are easily damaged by vehicles breaking through the desert surface and obliterating the figures."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7e178605-ecac-453e-9497-2f4bab7f4e45",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "Geoglyphs usually are found on fans sparse of rock, but what seems the oldest of the scraped designs are on cobbled terraces at Denning Springs, Ocotillo, Death Valley, and above the Mojave River."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ca044cec-37b0-4fbf-b2c2-b5a9c16de873",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "Two of the prehistoric sites, a quarry and a special task area, are common in the region, but the other site, a geoglyph, is rare and unique."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "69ef4366-e74e-4cd8-9701-e2837c46598c",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "Another important form of artistic expression during the ERD period, particularly on the south coast of Peru, was gigantic geoglyphs or man-made desert markings. The idea is ancient. [...] Large geoglyphs are found as far south as the far north coast of Chile. However, by far the largest and best-known concentration occur on the stony desert plains around the Río Grande de Nazca drainage (in a roughly 200-km² area). They are popularly known as the \"Nazca Lines.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2c8d2344-b7fa-4a94-abad-f4aed9329886",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "What a wonderful adventure, the making of a geoglyph. Geoglyphs include the largest images ever produced by prehistoric man. [...] A geoglyph [To Life (1999), a sculpture by Andrew Rogers] is unusual in the world and even more unusual in Israel where it is located in the wonderful and spectacular desert wilderness near the Dead Sea next to the Jordanian border."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "73a8df58-e8c7-4d2c-8010-0660325b16ce",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "Initially crop circles were just that, circles. However, over the years they have evolved into complex and elaborate pictograms – such as the recent ‘Mayan’ circle at West Kennet Long Barrow. Hence, some prefer to speak of them as ‘geoglyphs’. This shift from ‘crop circle’ to ‘geoglyph’ follows a change in their interpretation. Whereas the circle was understood simply to indicate alien presence, the pictogram has led to a belief in explicit communication."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cfb28559-4044-4c33-b2d9-a67ff4b242b0",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "The Palpa Valleys have also yielded petroglyphs and geoglyphs, which comprise the direct antecedents of the famous Nasca Lines."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "64d5749f-4b1b-4e89-8878-171fc5baacc1",
                                                "sense_id": "f1dd6b2e-d135-3e84-bfdd-e1b84f6f88b7",
                                                "text": "IBM Japan and Yamagata University last year presented the first geoglyph — a large human-made ancient formation or design on the ground — identified by AI."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8bf39307-2eeb-4b52-849b-9b65d9f1a0cf",
                        "source_entry_id": "b4918fd1-3c27-3c4b-94e5-49cd2f309d25",
                        "source_sense_id": "669800e4-4c87-332e-82ae-6f017e8eac8f",
                        "target_lang_word": "géoglyphe",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "géoglyphe",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5f35cc6d-4300-3634-8153-9f6070934e7e",
                                        "entry_id": "96628553-1e8a-399f-aa8e-f2dfce79d8b4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Grand dessin, grand motif à même le sol, visible depuis le ciel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Dessin"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8be20ad0-82f3-4897-8006-d3b40722b436",
                                                "sense_id": "5f35cc6d-4300-3634-8153-9f6070934e7e",
                                                "text": "Beaucoup plus étonnant, cependant, le fait que cette légende du nord du continent se retrouve illustrée à des dizaines de milliers de kilomètres, au sud du Pérou, dans le grand désert de Nazca. Là, en effet, parmi les géoglyphes tracés par cette mystérieuse civilisation qui naquit il y a plus de 2000 ans et s’épanouit entre le IVᵉ et VIᵉ siècle après J.-C., sur ce sol aride fait de rocaille et de sable, non loin du fameux colibri merveilleusement stylisé que l’on connaît et qui, de ses centre trente-cinq mètres de long, pointe son bec sur le solstice d’été (21 décembre), apparaît un second oiseau qui, ailes déployées, en transporte un autre plus petit sur son dos."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "32f19d10-d47b-443f-82dc-ca4f202d5cbf",
                                                "sense_id": "5f35cc6d-4300-3634-8153-9f6070934e7e",
                                                "text": "L’Amérique latine comporte un nombre record de sites tels que les géoglyphes de Nasca (Pérou), la ville de La Plata (Argentine) ou le centre historique de Salvador de Bahia (Brésil)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9bbb7a98-b814-4496-aaa4-9b66304afb22",
                                                "sense_id": "5f35cc6d-4300-3634-8153-9f6070934e7e",
                                                "text": "Le site de la ferme éolienne, équipée de technologie européenne, est dominé par un plateau où se trouve un géoglyphe de pierres en forme de roue à sept rayons indiquant les points cardinaux, qui sert encore à des cérémonies rituelles."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e92a981-e34c-43ef-bf48-a6df040c40af",
                                                "sense_id": "5f35cc6d-4300-3634-8153-9f6070934e7e",
                                                "text": "Pour débuter leurs travaux, Nicholas Saunders et Clive Ruggles ont tout d’abord décidé de différencier les étapes de la construction du géoglyphe."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
